long it actually will

00:08:58.800 --> 00:09:01.470
take to process all of these.

00:09:01.470 --> 00:09:04.186
So I'm going to run this query.

00:09:04.186 --> 00:09:06.060
And in addition to
pulling all those records,

00:09:06.060 --> 00:09:08.755
it's doing these aggregations
behind the scenes.

00:09:08.755 --> 00:09:10.380
And it's doing this
wild card operation

00:09:10.380 --> 00:09:13.170
to see the titles where
it's matching Google.

00:09:13.170 --> 00:09:15.980
And then it's showing the pages
that have the most views first.

00:09:15.980 --> 00:09:17.550
Now, the most interesting
thing, yeah, the results

00:09:17.550 --> 00:09:18.260
are down here.

00:09:18.260 --> 00:09:20.760
But the thing about what
makes BigQuery BigQuery is

00:09:20.760 --> 00:09:22.350
if you look here on
the right, you'll

00:09:22.350 --> 00:09:24.480
see that in about
11 or 12 seconds

00:09:24.480 --> 00:09:28.440
it processed through
416 gigabytes of data.

00:09:28.440 --> 00:09:30.560
So massive amounts of data.

00:09:30.560 --> 00:09:32.311
You just put your data
inside of BigQuery,

00:09:32.311 --> 00:09:34.226
and then run these ad
hoc queries on top of it

00:09:34.226 --> 00:09:36.090
without having to
worry about spinning up

00:09:36.090 --> 00:09:38.631
database servers, or managing
software, or anything like that

00:09:38.631 --> 00:09:39.641
behind the scenes.

00:09:39.641 --> 00:09:40.140
Interesting.

00:09:40.140 --> 00:09:41.970
It's also an exploration tool.

00:09:41.970 --> 00:09:44.620
So if you wanted to see, well,
what are the other public data

00:09:44.620 --> 00:09:46.971
sets that are available out
there inside of BigQuery,

00:09:46.971 --> 00:09:48.720
you could see that,
all right, well, there

00:09:48.720 --> 00:09:52.320
is some weather data from
NOAA going back to 1929,

00:09:52.320 --> 00:09:54.450
and a bunch of other
publicly available data sets.

00:09:54.450 --> 00:09:56.075
Because you might
say, all right, Evan,

00:09:56.075 --> 00:09:58.350
I don't have a data set
that I can play around with.

00:09:58.350 --> 00:10:01.710
Do you have example queries,
or do you have example data

00:10:01.710 --> 00:10:03.390
that I can just
get a feel for what

00:10:03.390 --> 00:10:04.860
are some of the different
things that I can do with SQL,

00:10:04.860 --> 00:10:06.360
and what are the
different types of things

00:10:06.360 --> 00:10:07.760
that I can do with the
data sources and data

00:10:07.760 --> 00:10:09.040
sets that are out there?

00:10:09.040 --> 00:10:10.320
So not only can you
query, but you can also

00:10:10.320 --> 00:10:12.111
explore some of the
really cool data that's

00:10:12.111 --> 00:10:14.809
available publicly, both
here inside the BigQuery UI.

00:10:14.809 --> 00:10:16.350
And there's a great
team that manages

00:10:16.350 --> 00:10:17.610
the BigQuery public data sets.

00:10:17.610 --> 00:10:19.610
So if you just Google
BigQuery public data sets,

00:10:19.610 --> 00:10:21.250
and we'll drop the
link into the bottom,

00:10:21.250 --> 00:10:22.580
you can see a lot of
really cool data sets

00:10:22.580 --> 00:10:23.870
that you can play around with.

00:10:23.870 --> 00:10:24.370
All right.

00:10:24.370 --> 00:10:26.780
So BigQuery, again,
you can ingest data.

00:10:26.780 --> 00:10:29.645
You query it live,
really fast, with inside

00:10:29.645 --> 00:10:30.600
of SQL that you write.

00:10:30.600 --> 00:10:32.160
You're going to
learn more about what

00:10:32.160 --> 00:10:33.840
are the different
types of advanced SQL

00:10:33.840 --> 00:10:36.173
that you can do inside of
BigQuery inside of the course.

00:10:36.173 --> 00:10:38.680
And, of course, you can run
your queries, save the results,

00:10:38.680 --> 00:10:40.871
store the results.

00:10:40.871 --> 00:10:41.370
All right.

00:10:41.370 --> 00:10:43.380
And as you see
here, the results.

00:10:43.380 --> 00:10:47.230
We have English for the
Google page that has--

00:10:47.230 --> 00:10:49.230
let me do my counting here--

00:10:49.230 --> 00:10:53.860
9 million views for
the English page.

00:10:53.860 --> 00:10:57.267
We have the Spanish page there
for the second result there.

00:10:57.267 --> 00:10:58.850
And you can imagine
that if you wanted

00:10:58.850 --> 00:11:00.450
to change any part
of your query,

00:11:00.450 --> 00:11:02.370
you just adjust the query here.

00:11:02.370 --> 00:11:05.130
And you can return the new
results there, as well.

00:11:05.130 --> 00:11:08.100
That's the tip of the iceberg
when it comes to BigQuery.

00:11:08.100 --> 00:11:09.411
You re-execute queries.

00:11:09.411 --> 00:11:11.160
One of the things that
I like to do almost

00:11:11.160 --> 00:11:14.310
as a trick for the
students in the course

00:11:14.310 --> 00:11:17.160
is if I reran it, do you think
it will run faster, or slower,

00:11:17.160 --> 00:11:18.245
or the same speed?

00:11:18.245 --> 00:11:20.370
A lot of people will say,
oh, it's the same, right?

00:11:20.370 --> 00:11:21.786
Well, the good
news about BigQuery

00:11:21.786 --> 00:11:23.640
is it'll actually store
your results, cached

00:11:23.640 --> 00:11:26.160
behind the scenes where it can.

00:11:26.160 --> 00:11:29.190
And this query, for example,
took three seconds cached.

00:11:29.190 --> 00:11:32.196
And it didn't query
416 gigabytes of data,

00:11:32.196 --> 00:11:33.570
because it had
already done that.

00:11:33.570 --> 00:11:35.070
So it's a little
bit of intelligence

00:11:35.070 --> 00:11:38.940
that's built in behind the
scenes inside the architecture.

00:11:38.940 --> 00:11:40.980
I could go on and talk
at length about BigQuery.

00:11:40.980 --> 00:11:42.600
But one of the
biggest things here

00:11:42.600 --> 00:11:44.850
is that this Wikipedia
data was already loaded,

00:11:44.850 --> 00:11:47.567
and more importantly,
already cleaned and cleansed.

00:11:47.567 --> 00:11:49.650
Now, I guarantee you the
data that you have inside

00:11:49.650 --> 00:11:52.320
of your organizations is
probably not only siloed

00:11:52.320 --> 00:11:55.520
in different departments,
but it is also uncleaned.

00:11:55.520 --> 00:11:57.510
So say the act of
preparing your data--

00:11:57.510 --> 00:11:59.940
converting dates to
the right date format,

00:11:59.940 --> 00:12:01.440
truncating fields--

00:12:01.440 --> 00:12:03.960
that's often a pain to do,
especially by hand, or even

00:12:03.960 --> 00:12:05.190
sometimes with SQL.

00:12:05.190 --> 00:12:08.380
And we're going to see next
what a better way to cleanse

00:12:08.380 --> 00:12:09.540
and explore that data is.

00:12:13.209 --> 00:12:14.250
JULIEN PHALIP: All right.

00:12:14.250 --> 00:12:16.485
Switching back to the slides.

00:12:19.830 --> 00:12:23.860
So Cloud Dataprep.

00:12:23.860 --> 00:12:28.930
As Evan just mentioned, to be
able to do valuable analytics

00:12:28.930 --> 00:12:30.580
or machine learning
algorithms, you

00:12:30.580 --> 00:12:33.970
need to have data
that's in a clean state.

00:12:33.970 --> 00:12:38.440
You need that data to conform
to some integrated rules.

00:12:38.440 --> 00:12:40.900
For example, the
data has to be valid.

00:12:40.900 --> 00:12:43.390
One example is that
numbers have to fall

00:12:43.390 --> 00:12:45.850
within the expected range.

00:12:45.850 --> 00:12:49.330
Dates have to be
properly formatted.

00:12:49.330 --> 00:12:51.220
Another example is
about completeness.

00:12:51.220 --> 00:12:53.307
You want to make sure
that you have all the data

00:12:53.307 --> 00:12:53.890
that you need.

00:12:53.890 --> 00:12:55.390
If there are some
missing values,

00:12:55.390 --> 00:12:57.670
you have to be able
to deal with that,

00:12:57.670 --> 00:13:00.880
and maybe fetch the proper
information to complete

00:13:00.880 --> 00:13:02.110
that data.

00:13:02.110 --> 00:13:04.430
Uniformity is also
an important role.

00:13:04.430 --> 00:13:06.430
You want to make sure
that, for example, numbers

00:13:06.430 --> 00:13:12.210
are in the right unit so that
you can do proper calculations.

00:13:12.210 --> 00:13:16.180
However, yes, it is true
that in the real world

00:13:16.180 --> 00:13:18.910
it's very rare
that you are handed

00:13:18.910 --> 00:13:20.870
a clean data set to begin with.

00:13:20.870 --> 00:13:23.170
So that's why you have
to do some transformation

00:13:23.170 --> 00:13:26.080
on that data to make it
cleaner so that then you

00:13:26.080 --> 00:13:30.570
can do proper analytics or
machine learning work with it.

00:13:30.570 --> 00:13:33.570
So this is where Cloud
Dataprep comes in.

00:13:33.570 --> 00:13:37.610
Cloud Dataprep is Google's
self service data wrangling

00:13:37.610 --> 00:13:41.250
tool, data wrangling being
the process of taking data

00:13:41.250 --> 00:13:45.540
in its native format, and then
making it usable for analysis.

00:13:45.540 --> 00:13:47.280
Dataprep is a UI based tool.

00:13:47.280 --> 00:13:48.850
There is no need
to write any code.

00:13:48.850 --> 00:13:50.470
And it's very easy to use.

00:13:50.470 --> 00:13:54.330
So this is what I'm going
to show you now in a demo.

00:13:58.330 --> 00:13:59.050
OK.

00:13:59.050 --> 00:14:00.690
So what you can see
here on the screen

00:14:00.690 --> 00:14:03.940
is a data set which I've just
loaded in Cloud Dataprep.

00:14:03.940 --> 00:14:09.170
It is a data set of about a
million records of fake credit

00:14:09.170 --> 00:14:11.030
card transactions.

00:14:11.030 --> 00:14:14.385
Those are fake just for
the purpose of this demo.

00:14:14.385 --> 00:14:16.980
And so I'm going to walk
