those transactions were made,

00:14:42.990 --> 00:14:44.980
you can see two spikes there.

00:14:44.980 --> 00:14:46.130
So that looks interesting.

00:14:46.130 --> 00:14:48.588
What's something you can do is
to look in the details here,

00:14:48.588 --> 00:14:52.140
in the current details,
to take a closer look.

00:14:52.140 --> 00:14:58.560
And yes, indeed, you can see
that there are two spikes here,

00:14:58.560 --> 00:15:01.169
two moments in time.

00:15:01.169 --> 00:15:01.710
I don't know.

00:15:01.710 --> 00:15:05.209
Maybe there was some marketing
campaign that happened.

00:15:05.209 --> 00:15:07.250
We are already starting
to get some insight here.

00:15:07.250 --> 00:15:12.810
It's something you might want
to look into further later on.

00:15:12.810 --> 00:15:15.930
You can see also Dataprep
shows you more information,

00:15:15.930 --> 00:15:20.610
like, for example, which day of
the week the data was recorded,

00:15:20.610 --> 00:15:23.480
or which month of the year.

00:15:23.480 --> 00:15:26.860
So, as you can see, you can
drill down into each column

00:15:26.860 --> 00:15:31.900
and then get some more
details about that data.

00:15:31.900 --> 00:15:34.980
Let's take a look at the
transaction amount column here.

00:15:34.980 --> 00:15:37.390
What you can see here
at the top is what

00:15:37.390 --> 00:15:40.570
is called the data quality bar.

00:15:40.570 --> 00:15:43.810
It gives you some
information about the quality

00:15:43.810 --> 00:15:46.000
of that particular column.

00:15:46.000 --> 00:15:51.100
Here you can see, in particular,
