00:17:43.950 --> 00:17:47.690
However, we also need to
tweak that transformation

00:17:47.690 --> 00:17:49.130
to also remove the dollar sign.

00:17:49.130 --> 00:17:53.330
So this is what I'm going
to do here by clicking Edit.

00:17:53.330 --> 00:17:55.520
And here in the pattern
that is [? matched, ?] I'm

00:17:55.520 --> 00:18:02.960
going to select USD, as
well as the dollar sign.

00:18:06.170 --> 00:18:07.130
And there you have it.

00:18:07.130 --> 00:18:09.980
The dollar sign is also
going to be removed.

00:18:09.980 --> 00:18:13.760
So now I can just add
this step in the recipe.

00:18:13.760 --> 00:18:19.555
A recipe is basically a
series of transformation steps

00:18:19.555 --> 00:18:22.051
that you want to
apply to a data set.

00:18:22.051 --> 00:18:22.550
All right.

00:18:22.550 --> 00:18:25.100
So now we've cleaned up the
transaction amount column.

00:18:25.100 --> 00:18:28.396
Let's move on and look
at the transaction type.

00:18:28.396 --> 00:18:31.430
The transaction
type column contains

00:18:31.430 --> 00:18:32.870
the type of the transaction.

00:18:32.870 --> 00:18:38.450
It could be a debit, or
credit, or an authorization.

00:18:38.450 --> 00:18:40.380
Something that could be
useful in some cases,

00:18:40.380 --> 00:18:42.080
for example in
machine learning, it's

00:18:42.080 --> 00:18:45.650
useful to create features
that will extract

00:18:45.650 --> 00:18:50.006
the different types of
the different values that

00:18:50.006 --> 00:18:51.630
are available in the
particular column.

00:18:51.630 --> 00:18:54.260
It's also useful for doing
aggregation calculation.

00:18:54.260 --> 00:18:58.220
So something we can do here
is to split that column

00:18:58.220 --> 00:19:02.610
into three separate
columns, one for each value.

00:19:02.610 --> 00:19:05.120
So down here.

00:19:12.784 --> 00:19:13.480
Oh, sorry.

