00:24:53.700 --> 00:24:55.680
Before we do that,
I just wanted to do

00:24:55.680 --> 00:24:57.910
a little bit of an exercise.

00:24:57.910 --> 00:24:59.400
Take a look at these two fruits.

00:24:59.400 --> 00:25:03.600
Even without machine learning,
just think about heuristically,

00:25:03.600 --> 00:25:06.972
how would you classify
them as one or the other?

00:25:06.972 --> 00:25:08.680
So a lot of you
might be thinking,

00:25:08.680 --> 00:25:10.760
OK, well, obviously I know,
just with my human intuition,

00:25:10.760 --> 00:25:12.093
the one on the left is an apple.

00:25:12.093 --> 00:25:13.860
The one on the
right's an orange.

00:25:13.860 --> 00:25:14.910
One of them's red.

00:25:14.910 --> 00:25:17.770
One of them's orange.

00:25:17.770 --> 00:25:20.550
Let's classify them
based on color.

00:25:20.550 --> 00:25:21.700
Not so easy.

00:25:21.700 --> 00:25:23.740
So now what happens if
I gave you these images?

00:25:27.190 --> 00:25:30.960
Now, what happens if
I added more fruit?

00:25:30.960 --> 00:25:33.540
See, on the right you
have not only something

00:25:33.540 --> 00:25:35.930
that's spherical in shape-ish.

00:25:35.930 --> 00:25:37.230
A little bit oblong there.

00:25:37.230 --> 00:25:39.420
That mango is also multi-color.

00:25:39.420 --> 00:25:41.850
So when you're doing
these hard-coded rules,

00:25:41.850 --> 00:25:43.590
or imagine building
a big decision

00:25:43.590 --> 00:25:47.160
tree of if this fruit is
red, then this, or maybe

00:25:47.160 --> 00:25:51.180
even if it has a stem,
you get into a very,

00:25:51.180 --> 00:25:55.050
dare I say, like hard-coded
fashion of a ton of rules.

00:25:55.050 --> 00:25:59.420
And that's maybe a
little bit too much

00:25:59.420 --> 00:26:00.920
that you wanted to take on.

00:26:00.920 --> 00:26:02.730
Well, let's start
with something easier.

00:26:02.730 --> 00:26:04.660
How would you classify,
say, for example,

00:26:04.660 --> 00:26:06.810
the difference between
this dog here and the mop?

00:26:06.810 --> 00:26:07.940
It's very easy to
know the difference

00:26:07.940 --> 00:26:09.231
between a dog and a mop, right?

00:26:09.231 --> 00:26:11.844
That's going to be just like
one of those 0 and 1 things.

00:26:11.844 --> 00:26:13.835
Oh, not so much.

00:26:13.835 --> 00:26:16.730
It turns out there is
a lot of dogs out there

00:26:16.730 --> 00:26:18.600
that look like mops.

00:26:18.600 --> 00:26:21.270
So this is just
an example there.

00:26:21.270 --> 00:26:24.320
So even for humans,
it's very hard.

00:26:24.320 --> 00:26:27.290
Just sometimes that might seem
like an easy classification

00:26:27.290 --> 00:26:31.000
task gets you quickly
into hot water.

00:26:31.000 --> 00:26:32.450
And the good news
is, with machine

00:26:32.450 --> 00:26:34.670
learning the
machines can pick up.

00:26:34.670 --> 00:26:37.031
And they don't learn
these hard-coded rules.

00:26:37.031 --> 00:26:38.780
So machine learning
is all about replacing

00:26:38.780 --> 00:26:41.300
these hard-coded rules
or heuristic rules

00:26:41.300 --> 00:26:44.330
with learned knowledge
from historical patterns

00:26:44.330 --> 00:26:45.350
that they've seen.

00:26:45.350 --> 00:26:47.630
So with Google
Photos, for example,

00:26:47.630 --> 00:26:49.190
you feed in a ton
of images of dogs

00:26:49.190 --> 00:26:50.356
and a ton of images of mops.

00:26:50.356 --> 00:26:53.330
When I say a ton, like
millions of records, billions

00:26:53.330 --> 00:26:54.860
of records of these images.

00:26:54.860 --> 00:26:56.360
And then the
algorithms, the machine

00:26:56.360 --> 00:26:59.720
learning models get smart enough
to then classify this as, say,

00:26:59.720 --> 00:27:04.100
99% sure that this is
a dog and not a mop.

00:27:04.100 --> 00:27:07.092
So the ultimate question there
is, how do you get access

00:27:07.092 --> 00:27:08.800
to these friendly
machine learning models

00:27:08.800 --> 00:27:11.210
where the training's
already been done?

00:27:11.210 --> 00:27:13.430
You can use those five.

00:27:13.430 --> 00:27:15.050
And that's just the
tip of the iceberg

00:27:15.050 --> 00:27:17.360
for some of the
pre-trained ML APIs, those

00:27:17.360 --> 00:27:18.410
five there on the right.

00:27:18.410 --> 00:27:19.910
And that's what
we're going to demo.

00:27:19.910 --> 00:27:21.380
Or if you're saying,
well, there's

00:27:21.380 --> 00:27:24.692
not really a pre-trained
ML API for my use case,

00:27:24.692 --> 00:27:26.150
that's when you
would use something

00:27:26.150 --> 00:27:28.610
like TensorFlow or
the cloud machine

00:27:28.610 --> 00:27:31.069
learning engine to write
your own model code,

00:27:31.069 --> 00:27:32.360
and then train your own models.

00:27:32.360 --> 00:27:34.602
But, again, the training
step for a model,

00:27:34.602 --> 00:27:36.560
and, more importantly,
getting that really rich

00:27:36.560 --> 00:27:40.960
historical data, is oftentimes
one of the hardest efforts.

