00:42:15.660 --> 00:42:16.620
You can play around.

00:42:16.620 --> 00:42:20.400
And you can preview the effect
of your transformations.

00:42:20.400 --> 00:42:22.950
And sometimes it's
actually enough to do

00:42:22.950 --> 00:42:24.330
simple transformations.

00:42:24.330 --> 00:42:27.365
And, again, if you have
more complex, more specific

00:42:27.365 --> 00:42:29.430
or granular transformation
you want to do,

00:42:29.430 --> 00:42:32.260
then this is where you
want to use DataFlow.

00:42:32.260 --> 00:42:32.969
EVAN JONES: Cool.

00:42:32.969 --> 00:42:35.426
Last but not least, what are
the performance considerations

00:42:35.426 --> 00:42:36.790
for BigQuery data sets?

00:42:36.790 --> 00:42:39.750
So we have an entire module
of the course dedicated

00:42:39.750 --> 00:42:41.629
to making your queries
high performance

00:42:41.629 --> 00:42:43.420
and making your data
sets high performance.

00:42:43.420 --> 00:42:45.330
But just off the top
of my head, anytime

00:42:45.330 --> 00:42:46.500
you're doing
transformations, make

00:42:46.500 --> 00:42:48.541
sure you're only using
the data that you actually

00:42:48.541 --> 00:42:49.440
want to transform.

00:42:49.440 --> 00:42:51.540
That means if you
can filter your data

00:42:51.540 --> 00:42:54.720
with a where clause early,
filter early and filter often.

00:42:54.720 --> 00:42:57.519
Also, if you're a beginner
to intermediate SQL,

00:42:57.519 --> 00:42:59.310
there are some really
advanced SQL concepts

00:42:59.310 --> 00:43:01.268
that we go through in
detail inside the course,

00:43:01.268 --> 00:43:03.750
like un-nesting or nesting
data, where you can actually

00:43:03.750 --> 00:43:09.836
have a table that has inside
of it to drill down records.

00:43:09.836 --> 00:43:11.460
So if you have a
customer, and then you

00:43:11.460 --> 00:43:13.220
have all the orders
associated with them,

00:43:13.220 --> 00:43:14.940
normal relational
database schema

00:43:14.940 --> 00:43:16.340
would say, separate the two.

00:43:16.340 --> 00:43:18.006
But inside of BigQuery,
you can actually

00:43:18.006 --> 00:43:20.191
store parent-child records
within the same table.

00:43:20.191 --> 00:43:21.690
And you get the
performance benefits

00:43:21.690 --> 00:43:24.240
of automatically
pre-joining the tables

00:43:24.240 --> 00:43:26.920
without having to do an
expensive SQL joint operation.

